## 6. Understanding Docker Images

A Docker image contains the filesystem and configuration needed to create a container.

Images can be downloaded from registries, built from Dockerfiles, and shared with other environments.

### 6.1 List Local Images

```bash
docker image ls
```

### 6.2 Download an Image

```bash
docker pull nginx:latest
```

Downloads the selected image from the default registry, Docker Hub, unless another registry is specified.

### 6.3 Inspect an Image

```bash
docker image inspect nginx:latest
```

Shows image metadata, architecture, configuration, and other details.

### 6.4 View Image History

```bash
docker image history nginx:latest
```

Displays the recorded image build history and associated layer information.

### 6.5 Remove an Unused Image

```bash
docker image rm nginx:latest
```

Docker may reject the removal if the image is still referenced by a container.

**Note:** Removing an image is different from removing a container. Check what depends on an image before deleting it.

